//! Fixed output meanings exposed without opening a repository.
pub const ROOT: &str = "OUTPUT\nBusiness JSON is one document: ok, cmd, context, and data (normal report) or error (failure). context.mode identifies the mode; help is an executable explanation entry; summary describes this invocation; continuations contains purpose/command pairs, or [] when no action is known. ok=true means a normal report, NOT that every operation succeeded. Exit 0 may include warnings; 1 reports errors or partial results; 2 usage/environment; 3 human decision; 4 busy. ok=false does not imply rollback. error.code is the stable category; message explains the cause; optional detail is refusal-specific information. Unknown effects must be checked before retrying. --help and --version remain plain text, exit 0, even with --json.";
pub const QUERY: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. Text: count is returned hits, not total matches; results are top-N over indexed pages. score is relevance, not credibility; summary/snippet are not full text. Page/source modes expose literal relations, not semantic support or causality. Resolved total excludes unresolved links; unresolved links are not paginated. Sources refer to frontmatter raw references; a #fragment is not a read offset, and reverse lookup covers the entire raw. File presence does not prove readability or correctness. Page references accept a bare id, type/id, or a full page key; missing and ambiguous references are errors, not empty groups. Each relation group states requested (false means not requested), total (resolved items before paging), offset/limit, the returned items, next_offset, truncated, and next_command (null at the end); text hits are top-N and a truncated group's next_command continues it. sources are frontmatter references and are not paginated, so --offset/--limit do not apply to them. --graph describes the whole indexed wikilink structure, not a semantic evidence network.";
pub const INGEST: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. A round reports conversions and outstanding work, not automatic knowledge completion. write_to is the writable page tree, types the allowed page types, schema the contract file. changes lists every classified source change; ingested identifies sources that produced work this round; converted counts conversions and is not the merged page list. stale_pages lists pages turned stale because a cited source vanished. effects identifies actual merged/removed pages and confirmed sources. remaining and issues must be read even when next_task is null. A dry run is a preview, not proof of completion; initial provisioning and preview chunk caches may write. Parsed heads are not full documents. Chunk numbers and A-B ranges are 1-based inclusive. events, warnings and failures name the affected sources; counts alone do not. A delivered task's affected_pages is the union of pages referenced by that work set, not the current task's semantic scope.";
pub const READ: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. reference is an engine reference, not a source-file path or fragment selector. content is the requested line window from line_start to line_end; offset/limit describe that request, and total_lines describes the whole file. truncated says lines exist after line_end. Empty files have a 0/0 window; a window beyond EOF is empty, not a read error. Use context.continuations for the next executable read.";
pub const DOCTOR: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. checks contains name, level and natural-language detail. Ok passes that check; Warn calls attention but may exit 0; Fail exits 1. --only limits coverage, --key limits applicable source checks. --fix repairs shared engine state first, for every family, then reports; --only narrows the checks that follow, not the repair. Repair is mechanical, not semantic correctness, and repair records can coexist with later checks.";
pub const IGNORE: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. Listing: patterns are ordered rules, count is their number. Changes: added/removed describe rule changes only, not source/page cleanup. Check: key is the tested source key; ignored is the decision; pattern is the final matching rule, including !negative rules, or null when no rule matches. A non-null pattern does not necessarily mean ignored.";
pub const TASK: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. Read the action's --help for its report. Task closure, page merge and source settlement are separate facts; no current/next task does not prove the whole repository is complete.";
pub const TASK_DONE: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. closed identifies the submitted task. outcome is a summary, not a substitute for effects/remaining/issues. effects.merged_pages are actually merged pages; confirmed_sources are settled sources; removed_pages are actual removals. remaining counts known actionable/waiting/failed/blocked work. next_task is one delivery with its own guide, contract and examples, or null. A leaf may close while root settlement still fails.";
pub const TASK_STATUS: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. This observes without advancing tasks. roots lists source trees and open children; current is a deliverable task or null; failed/blocked retain tasks needing attention. Read current task's guide and contract before submitting a payload. Empty current does not prove there are no failed or blocked tasks.";
pub const TASK_RETRY: &str = "OUTPUT\nSee llm-wiki --help for the envelope. This human recovery action does not execute work or prove its cause is fixed. convert retries clear the failure record and delete the counter task; the returned old row is not a live task. pending/active rows are returned unchanged. Other applicable rows reset pending. --replan rebuilds a root route without reverting already merged pages.";
